Candidate class analysis 
| Candidate Concept | Candidate Class? | Reason |
|-------------------|------------------|--------|
| Patient           | Yes              | Core entity with attributes and behaviours |
| Practitioner      | Yes              | Core entity with availability and identity |
| Appointment       | Yes              | Central scheduling entity linking patient + practitioner |
| Name              | No               | Attribute of Patient/Practitioner, not a standalone class |
| Clinic            | Maybe            | Could represent system-level aggregation; optional |
| Database          | No               | Technical implementation detail, not domain model |
| Cancellation      | No               | Event or status change, not a standalone class |
| Status            | No               | Attribute of Appointment |

CRC Cards: Class responsibility collaborator
Patient:
Responsibilities:
- Store patient details
- Provide patient identification
- Link to appointment history
Collaborators:
- Appointment

Practitioner:
Responsibilities:
- Store practitioner details
- Maintain availability
- View schedule
Collaborators:
- Appointment

Appointment:
Responsibilities:
- Store appointment time, date, status
- Link patient and practitioner
- Update status (booked, cancelled, completed)
Collaborators:
- Patient
- Practitioner

Relationship reasoning:
Patient - Appointment
Relationship: One to many - A patient can have many appointments
Reason: Patients attened multiple consultations over time

Practioner - Appointment 
Multiplicity: One to many - A practinoner handles many appointment 
Reason: Practioner see multiple patients daily

Should Appointment inherit from Patient?
No.
Reason:  
Inheritance represents “is-a” relationships.
An appointment is not a patient — it links a patient and practitioner.
Correct relationship: association, not inheritance.

Does Clinic need to own every object?
Optional.

Reason:  
A “Clinic” class can act as a system root (aggregate), but it is not required.
The domain model works fine with Patient, Practitioner, and Appointment as primary classes.

AI Model Critique (Stage 3)
Critique the following AI‑proposed classes:

PatientManager
Issue: Manager classes are implementation details, not domain concepts.
Verdict: Reject for domain model; acceptable later in software architecture.

PractitionerManager:
Same reasoning as above.
Reject for domain model.

AppointmentManager:
Represents system behaviour, not domain entity.
Reject for domain model.

ClinicController:
“Controller” is a UI/logic layer concept.
Reject for domain model.

NotificationManager:
SmartCare v0.2 does not include notifications.
Reject — out of scope.

ScheduleEngine:
Over-engineered for a small clinic.
Reject — not part of the domain model.

Final domain model:
Classes: 
- Patient
- Practioner 
- Appointment

Attributes:
Patient: id, name, contact
Practitioner: id, name, availability
Appointment: id, date, time, status, patientID, practitionerID

Relationships:
Patient 1..* Appointment
Practitioner 1..* Appointment - Appointment links both